Subject: [PATCH] Report output decoded before an error, return the error next

Decompressor::read returned Err as soon as the state machine hit invalid
data, dropping the count of the bytes it had written in the same call. The
state machine keeps going after the output buffer is full as long as no
output is needed (block headers, code lengths, the checksum), so whether
corrupt data right after the caller's last needed byte is reported depends
on how the input is split into chunks: given all input at once, read() fails
on an invalid block header that follows the data; given it byte by byte,
read() returns the data first and a caller that stops once its output is
full (the png crate stops at the end of the frame) never sees the error.
Found by the png `buf_independent` fuzzer (whole-slice reader fails with
InvalidBlockType / InvalidUncompressedBlockLength / BadCodeLengthHuffmanTree
/ InvalidHlit / InvalidHdist, byte-by-byte readers succeed).

Like std::io::Read, return the output written before the error with Ok and
keep the error for the next call. Errors in a call that wrote nothing are
returned directly as before. Valid streams decode exactly as before. One
visible change for corrupt streams: decompress_to_vec_bounded() reports
OutputTooLarge instead of the error when the corruption follows exactly
maxlen bytes of output. Present at fdeflate HEAD a713d02 too (read()
lost its end_of_input argument there; the change is the same).
Test: test_input_chunking_sensitivity_error_after_full_output.

